/*
:name: mem_ordering_none
:type: equiv
:verilog_top: mem_ordering_none
:pyrope_top: mem_ordering_none
*/
// `ordering="none"` showcase (lhdsuite array_problem.md, ruled 2026-07-27;
// really UNDEFINED since 2026-07-29): no ordering hardware at all — a read of
// an address written this same cycle is UNDEFINED (simulation: random, since
// Slop has no x; emitted Verilog: x; formal: a don't-care, i.e. either value is
// acceptable). Reads of non-written addresses return the stored value as usual.
//
// The mode used to be REFINED to "read the committed value", which made it
// bit-identical to the new `ordering="old"` and pinned exactly the value it is
// supposed to leave free. It now drives the Memory cell's `undef` matrix
// (graph/cell.cpp pid 15): cgen passes it as the wrapper's UNDEF parameter and
// the cvc5 encoder turns it into an X bit-plane on the read dout, so the miter
// excludes the collision window on the REFERENCE side. The golden .v below
// encodes the same window as x for the yosys path.
//
// This pair therefore proves for ANY refinement — committed-value, forwarding,
// or anything else — while still pinning the defined (non-colliding) behavior.
// The two-way freedom is what `//lhd/tests:lec_mem_undef_test` asserts directly;
// here it is what makes the v2prp direction green (the golden's x is on the
// IMPL side, and only a ref-side plane can excuse it). The user-visible payoff
// of "none" is cheapest hardware + the formal don't-care that lets equivalence
// prove when collision values genuinely do not matter (in which case program
// ordering was never needed).
pub mod mem_ordering_none(clk:u1, a1:u2, we:u1, a2:u2, d2:u2, a4:u2) -> (o1:u2@[], o4:u2@[]) {
  // `= nil` (no initializer) ON PURPOSE: an array init is a RESET VALUE
  // (restored by a one-entry-per-cycle sweep), and this pair is about
  // same-cycle ORDERING only — the golden below declares the array with no
  // power-on contents and no reset, exactly like this.
  reg mem:[4]u2:[ordering="none"]
  o1 = mem[a1]
  if we != 0 {
    mem[a2] = d2
  }
  o4 = mem[a4]
}
